WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.380
Run a catering business that is safe and profitable.

2
00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:11.240
This is the video for Module 5 of Advanced Cooking and Nutrition: running a home catering and food products business.

3
00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:20.680
Many caterers lose clients not because the food was poor, but because the queue was long, or food ran out on one side of the hall.

4
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:26.300
So you will plan staff and equipment, and handle gas safely at every venue.

5
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.760
You will cost each plate properly, so every order protects your profit.

6
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:39.900
And you will build the records and registrations that win contracts informal competitors cannot bid for.

7
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.610
For plated service, plan about one server for every 10 to 15 guests.

8
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.270
At a staffed buffet, about one server for every 25 to 30 guests.

9
00:00:52.520 --> 00:01:08.910
Plan one buffet line for every 75 to 100 guests, served from both sides if possible. For example, Patience in Uyo set 3 double-sided lines for 280 buffet guests, and quoted for 17 staff instead of guessing.

10
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.440
Always have at least one supervisor who does not serve.

11
00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:22.580
Write a run sheet: when the van leaves, when lines open, when the team must leave. Share it on your team WhatsApp group the day before.

12
00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:36.590
Keep gas cylinders upright, outdoors or well ventilated, away from the flame and from guests. Check hoses and connections with soapy water. Bubbles mean a leak. Never use a lighter to check.

13
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.590
Keep a fire extinguisher and fire blanket at the cooking area.

14
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:50.120
If you smell gas, turn off the cylinder valve, put out all flames, do not switch electrical items on or off, and move people away.

15
00:01:51.370 --> 00:02:02.200
Hot food should stay above 60 C during service. Check it with your probe thermometer on arrival. Throw away anything out of temperature control for more than 2 hours.

16
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:12.160
When you buy yam, plantain or fish, peel, bones and trimmings are lost. First, weigh what you buy. That is the as purchased weight.

17
00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:18.460
Then trim, cook and weigh what you can actually serve. That is the edible portion.

18
00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:25.150
Divide edible by purchased and multiply by 100 to get your yield percentage.

19
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.650
Your figures will change with season and supplier, so test your main ingredients yourself.

20
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:42.630
Then divide the portion you need by the yield. For example, 200 g of fried yam at 80% yield means buying 250 g.

21
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:58.430
Here is a worked example. Chioma serves 200 g of fried yam and 150 g of fish. Her yields are 80% for yam and 60% for fish, so she must buy 250 g of each.

22
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.760
At about 1,600 naira per kg, the yam costs 400 naira.

23
00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:10.550
At about 4,000 naira per kg, the fish costs 1,000 naira.

24
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.870
Sauce, oil and seasoning add 300 naira.

25
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:32.250
Food cost per plate is 1,700 naira. Divide by 0.35 and the price is 4,857 naira, so she sets 5,000. The rest must cover packaging, gas, staff, transport and profit.

26
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:41.140
Contribution margin is selling price minus food cost. Sort every dish by how many sold and by its margin.

27
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:52.860
A star is popular with a high margin, so keep it exactly the same. A plowhorse is popular with a low margin. Raise the price slightly or cut a costly component.

28
00:03:54.110 --> 00:04:08.770
A puzzle has a high margin but few sales, so photograph it better and recommend it. A dog is unpopular and low margin. Remove or redesign it. Repeat this every month or two, because prices change quickly.

29
00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:23.390
Register your business with CAC and file annual returns. Packaged products like spice blends need NAFDAC registration, so get requirements from NAFDAC directly, not social media agents.

30
00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:31.510
Ask your local government about health permits. Keep supplier, temperature, cleaning and complaints records, written at the time.

31
00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:36.270
Use a written agreement for every event, and never claim a food cures anything.

32
00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:45.010
To remember. Plan staff and lines from guest numbers. Treat gas with respect. Keep hot food above 60 C.

33
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:52.720
Price each plate from its true food cost. And keep records and written agreements, because they protect you and your clients.

34
00:04:53.970 --> 00:05:06.470
Your practice: plan staff, equipment and a run sheet for a 150-guest event, cost and engineer your own menu, and build your compliance pack with record templates and a client agreement.

35
00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.760
Continue with the lessons below.
